## Is back pain a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Short spells of back pain are not usually covered, but long-term back or neck pain that makes everyday tasks hard can be.

## Staying in work

Staying at work, or getting back to work early with the right support, usually helps recovery. Your GP can use a **fit note** to suggest changes, such as shorter hours or lighter duties.

## Reasonable adjustments

Your employer should consider changes such as:

- a supportive chair, sit-stand desk or workstation assessment
- avoiding heavy lifting, or providing lifting equipment
- more breaks, or the chance to move around
- a phased return after time off
- working from home on some days

**Access to Work** can help pay for equipment or support that goes beyond reasonable adjustments.

Your employer also has legal duties to reduce the risk of injury from **manual handling**.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how your condition affects you on most days. Long-term back pain or sciatica can affect activities such as:

- **moving around**, if pain stops you walking more than a short distance reliably
- **washing and bathing**, and **dressing**, if you cannot bend to reach your feet or get in and out of the bath
- **preparing food**, if you cannot stand at a cooker for long

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
